  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 1031 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nThe long term forecast will begin with a dry and warmer Saturday \nbefore a slight cool down and the return of daily precipitation \nchances. The upper ridging is expected to expand across more of the \nCentral Plains on Sunday. Temperatures will continue to warm with \nhighs in the upper 80s across the far southwestern Texas Panhandle \nto upper 90s across the southern Rolling Plains. Subsidence below \nthe ridging will keep the region dry. An upper pattern change will \nbring a cool down and daily precipitation chances on Monday. An \nupper low will dig down the coast of California before moving \nonshore off the coast of Baja California late Monday through early \nTuesday. This pattern change will shift to southwesterly flow aloft \npumping in subtropical moisture. Around the same time, Hurricane \nPolo is expected to make landfall off the coast of Baja Mexico. \nSouthwesterly flow will pull any residual moisture from that system. \nForcing from multiple passing shortwaves and plenty of moisture will \nbring daily precipitation chances with the greatest chances looking \nto be on Tuesday. Models are in disagreement in the progression of \nthe upper low for the latter half of the week. ECMWF has the upper \nlow stalling across the region through Friday before gradually \ndeamplifying and being absorbed into northerly flow over the \nweekend. GFS has the low getting absorbed into northerly flow by the \nend of the work week as an upper trough amplifies across western \nCONUS over the weekend.",
